<p class='text'>I finally made it to New Zealand!</p>
<p class='text'>As a big rugby fan, I've wanted to go to the other side of the world for a good long time now (I won't claim as long as I can remember, but at least since I was a teenager).  And since it's been possible to watch domestic NZ rugby (2016 in my case), I have become even more desperate to go.  I'd decided to come in 2020, only for the pandemic to stop all such plans in their tracks.  In retrospect, I'm very glad that I hadn't come and then got stuck here during lockdown.</p>
<p class='text'>It's also a welcome break from everything else that's been going on.  Don't get me wrong - it is a delight having grandchildren and seeing them so often.  It's actually a lot of fun and a lot more stimulating than I was expecting, but it still isn't my skillset and I find it very hard work a lot of the time.  All the logistics are hard too - picking them up, dropping them off; even just getting into their building where we don't have a key and there isn't a working buzzer.</p>
<p class='text'>New Zealand is an interesting cross between the UK and the US.  Obviously, driving on the left is just like the UK, as is the frequent use of roundabouts and the much less frequent occurrence of stop signs and traffic lights.  On the other hand, the amount of space here - and the lack of people - is much more like the US.  And the scenery has that same dramatic quality to it.</p>
<p class='text'>I've been to a bunch of botanical gardens.  Everywhere I go, people tell me I should have come a couple of months ago, in the summer.  I would have loved too, but this is the time I could get away and so I have to make do with the lesser displays.</p>
<p class='text'>There have been some surprises too.  We've been going to Jamaica Blue - a cafe in the Arndale in Manchester - a lot, and I was surprised to find there was a cafe with the same name in a shopping centre in Hamilton.  I was even more surprised to find that it was the <i>same</i> chain and, furthermore, that said chain was from Australia.  Live and learn, I suppose.  I think there's a moral in there somewhere if you have what it takes to go looking for it.</p>
<p class='text'>The rugby has not been disappointing.  I've seen five games - two featuring "my" Chiefs - and have one more to go on Friday before I fly back to the US on Saturday, and I've been to three of the five "big" stadia, with one more to come on Friday.  It's only Christchurch that I'll be missing out on.</p>
<p class='text'>The weather has also seemed a bit extreme.  I'm not sure whether that's normal or not.  I've been baking hot a lot of the time, but I've been drenched watching two separate rugby games.  It was amazing to watch the skilful handling of both the Chiefs and the Drua playing a game in torrential rain; a game played in those conditions in England would have just been a procession of knock-ons and scrums.</p>
<p class='text'>I've also managed to do some work while I've been here.  As I say, it's great looking after our grandchildren, but it doesn't leave me with the energy and focus to write code, so it's nice having been able to do that for a change.</p>
